    "result_text": "Done. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0`).\n\n## What changed\n\nVersion 2 of the owner identity, keeping the plain region code user-facing and the `V2` tag confined to the identifiers.\n\n**New: `Region.java`** \u2014 extracted the plain region derivation (postcode 2xxx/3xxx/4xxx \u2192 NSW/VIC/QLD, then city, else UNKNOWN) into one small class. This is the untagged region behind `locality`, `timezone` and the owner segment. Previously that logic lived inline in `AssignCustomerCode`, and `locality` was scraped back out of the memberId prefix \u2014 which no longer works once the prefix carries `V2`, so the two are now cleanly decoupled.\n\n**Identifiers rederived with the `V2` tag (every value changes, none can coincide with v1):**\n- `AssignCustomerCode` \u2014 the region code *inside* the memberId is now `\"V2\" + Region.of(owner)` (e.g. `V2NSW26\u2026`). No v1 memberId starts with `V2`, so they're provably disjoint.\n- `IdentityKey.key(...)` \u2014 hash input prefixed with `V2|`, so `identityKey` changes.\n- `IdentityKey.household(...)` \u2014 hash input prefixed with `V2|`, so `householdId` changes.\n\n**Response shape (`openapi.yml` + `OwnerMapper`):** the three identifiers moved into a nested `OwnerIdentity` object (`identity.memberId` / `.householdId` / `.identityKey`), removed from the top level, and a top-level integer `apiVersion: 2` added. `locality`/`timezone`/`ownerSegment` now derive from `Region.of(owner)` (plain, never tagged).\n\n**Audit (`AuditOwnerCreated`):** the structured event gains `\"schemaVersion\":2` and an `ownerSegment` recomputed via `OwnerSegment.of(level, Region.of(owner))`.\n\nRequest fields are untouched. Duplicate/household detection stays consistent because both existing owners and new requests run through the same v2 derivations. The observed audit line confirms it end-to-end: `memberId=V2NSW27\u2026`, `\"schemaVersion\":2`, `\"ownerSegment\":\"STANDARD_METRO\"` (METRO from the plain `NSW`, no `V2`).\n\nStructural impact was kept low: each identity change is a one-line edit to a small, single-method class, and the shared region logic was factored into one new cohesive unit rather than growing `AssignCustomerCode` or the mapper.",
